// Type-checking diagnostic codes.
//
// Subset 1 (meta/SUBSET_1.md). Cycle 0.4.1.
//
// Centralised for the reason 0.1.6, 0.2.1 and 0.3.1 give: a mistyped code
// literal at a call site silently invents a NEW code, and the test asserting the
// old one then fails for a reason unrelated to the bug it guards.

pub func:TYPE_UNKNOWN = string() never fails        { pass "NITPICK-TYPE-001"; };
pub func:TYPE_NOT_A_TYPE = string() never fails     { pass "NITPICK-TYPE-002"; };
pub func:TYPE_BARE_ANY = string() never fails       { pass "NITPICK-TYPE-003"; };
// NOT A COMPILE-TIME CONSTANT. One code for one rule, and the rule reaches two
// spellings: an array size `int32[N]` and a `comptime` generic argument
// `Mutex<Config, 2>`. Both are refused for the same reason and with the same
// sentence, so a reader filtering on this is asking one question -- splitting it
// in two would make the shared rule look like a coincidence.
pub func:TYPE_NOT_CONSTANT = string() never fails   { pass "NITPICK-TYPE-004"; };
pub func:TYPE_SELF_OUTSIDE = string() never fails   { pass "NITPICK-TYPE-005"; };
pub func:TYPE_DYN_NOT_TRAIT = string() never fails  { pass "NITPICK-TYPE-006"; };
pub func:TYPE_MISMATCH = string() never fails       { pass "NITPICK-TYPE-007"; };
pub func:TYPE_NOT_NUMERIC = string() never fails    { pass "NITPICK-TYPE-008"; };
pub func:TYPE_LOSSY_CAST = string() never fails     { pass "NITPICK-TYPE-009"; };
pub func:TYPE_RESULT_RETURN = string() never fails  { pass "NITPICK-TYPE-010"; };

// A DEFECT IN THE COMPILER, not in the program: a statement kind the checker's
// walk has no entry for. Its own code, and not `TYPE_UNKNOWN`, because a reader
// who sees it has found a hole in the checker and there is nothing they can
// change in their source to fix it. `resolve.npk` has `RESOLVE_INTERNAL` for the
// same situation and for the same reason.
pub func:TYPE_INTERNAL = string() never fails       { pass "NITPICK-TYPE-011"; };

// Traits and impls (cycle 0.4.6). Each is its own code rather than a shared
// "bad impl", because the four are found at different points and a reader
// filtering a log by code is asking a different question with each.
pub func:TYPE_NOT_A_TRAIT = string() never fails    { pass "NITPICK-TYPE-012"; };
pub func:TYPE_COHERENCE = string() never fails      { pass "NITPICK-TYPE-013"; };
pub func:TYPE_IMPL_INCOMPLETE = string() never fails{ pass "NITPICK-TYPE-014"; };
pub func:TYPE_NOT_OBJECT_SAFE = string() never fails{ pass "NITPICK-TYPE-015"; };

// Generics (cycle 0.4.7). Three codes rather than one, because the three are
// found at different points and a reader filtering by code is asking a different
// question with each: an arity mistake is in the annotation, an unsatisfied bound
// is in the argument, and a depth overflow is in the shape of the program.
pub func:TYPE_GENERIC_ARITY = string() never fails  { pass "NITPICK-TYPE-016"; };
pub func:TYPE_BOUND_UNMET = string() never fails    { pass "NITPICK-TYPE-017"; };
pub func:TYPE_GENERIC_DEPTH = string() never fails  { pass "NITPICK-TYPE-018"; };

// THREE FINDINGS THAT WERE ALL `TYPE_MISMATCH` OR `TYPE_GENERIC_ARITY`, and are
// not mismatches at all. Codes are the stable identifiers a test may assert on
// (BUILD_REFERENCE 7.1), so a distinction that lives only in the wording is a
// distinction nothing can check -- and each of these is a different mistake with
// a different fix.
//
//   * NO SUCH METHOD -- the receiver has no member by that name. On a generic
//     parameter this is the whole "no duck typing" property: without its own
//     code it reads identically to the argument mismatch that arises when the
//     free function IS wrongly reached.
//   * AMBIGUOUS -- two traits or two bounds supply one name. The program is
//     well-formed and the compiler will not choose (D-102).
//   * WRONG KIND OF GENERIC ARGUMENT -- a type where a `comptime` value was
//     wanted, or the reverse. Not arity: the count is right.
pub func:TYPE_NO_METHOD = string() never fails      { pass "NITPICK-TYPE-019"; };
pub func:TYPE_AMBIGUOUS_METHOD = string() never fails{ pass "NITPICK-TYPE-020"; };
pub func:TYPE_GENERIC_KIND = string() never fails   { pass "NITPICK-TYPE-021"; };

// INFERENCE COULD NOT DECIDE. Its own code because the fix is unlike any
// other: nothing in the call is wrong, and what is missing is a type argument
// the turbofish would have supplied.
pub func:TYPE_CANNOT_INFER = string() never fails   { pass "NITPICK-TYPE-022"; };

// A C VARIADIC `extern` FUNCTION, whose tail has no type the checker can name.
//
// Its own code because it is neither a mismatch nor a rung the backend cannot
// lower (D-085) — it is a signature shape the LANGUAGE has not settled. A Nitpick
// variadic writes `..*T[]:rest` and every trailing argument is checked against
// `T`; a C `..*` names nothing, and the obvious filler is `any[]`, which the same
// type system refuses as a bare `any` (TYPE-003) on purpose.
//
// Refused rather than guessed at. What stood here refused these calls too — as
// "`printf` is a type, not a value", because an extern function had no type at
// all — so this replaces a misleading refusal with an accurate one, and makes the
// open question visible instead of silently wrong.
pub func:TYPE_EXTERN_VARIADIC = string() never fails{ pass "NITPICK-TYPE-023"; };

// AN OPERAND THAT NEEDED AN ADDRESS AND HAS NONE. `@`, `$$i`, `$$m` and
// `move(place)` all take a place, and the language has no way to spell the address
// of a temporary.
//
// Its own code and not `TYPE_MISMATCH`, because nothing is wrong with the operand's
// TYPE — `@(raw f())` fails on `f()` having no home, and telling somebody their
// `int32` is not an `int32` would send them looking in the wrong direction.
pub func:TYPE_NOT_A_PLACE = string() never fails    { pass "NITPICK-TYPE-024"; };

// COMPILE-TIME EVALUATION DID NOT FINISH. Its own code rather than
// `TYPE_NOT_CONSTANT`, because they send the reader to opposite places: one means
// "this is not a constant expression", the other means "it is, and it never ends".
pub func:TYPE_COMPTIME_BUDGET = string() never fails { pass "NITPICK-TYPE-025"; };

// A STRUCT LITERAL NAMING A FIELD THE TYPE DOES NOT HAVE (0.6.7). Its own code
// rather than `TYPE_MISMATCH`, because the two are answered differently: a
// mismatched value is fixed by changing the value, and a field that does not exist
// is fixed by changing the name or the struct.
pub func:TYPE_NO_SUCH_FIELD = string() never fails  { pass "NITPICK-TYPE-026"; };

// A STRUCT LITERAL THAT DOES NOT NAME EVERY FIELD. There is no field-default
// syntax, so an omitted field has no value — and an unassigned binding is what
// D-010 refuses everywhere else. A safety property rather than an ergonomic one.
pub func:TYPE_MISSING_FIELD = string() never fails { pass "NITPICK-TYPE-027"; };

// THE SAME FIELD GIVEN TWICE. Taking the last silently discards the first, which is
// the lost-value defect `Result{value: a, value: b}` gets its own code for.
pub func:TYPE_FIELD_TWICE = string() never fails   { pass "NITPICK-TYPE-028"; };

// NITPICK-TYPE-029 lived here: "a construct the frontend accepts and no tier
// supplies a type for" — the `vec2(…)` constructor refusal from 0.6.7.
// RETIRED at 1.3.1: D-135 settled `simd<T, N>` as the mechanism (the vector
// family became library types over it), and the constructor now TYPES,
// annotation-directed like `channel()`. An un-annotated `simd(…)` refuses as
// TYPE-001 with the annotation rule; nothing else was ever Tier-refused.
// The number is not reused.

// D-143's float rules: a flt32 literal past 15 significant digits, or any
// literal/arithmetic/comparison on the storage-only flt128.
pub func:TYPE_FLOAT_RULES = string() never fails   { pass "NITPICK-TYPE-030"; };

// D-148: a literal whose value does not fit its type -- suffixed or contextual.
// Until 0.9.9 nothing checked this anywhere: `int8:a = 300i8;` survived to the
// backend and emitted `i8 300`, which is not even valid IR.
pub func:TYPE_LIT_RANGE = string() never fails     { pass "NITPICK-TYPE-031"; };

// THERE IS NO SUCH CONVERSION (1.0.8). Split from `TYPE_LOSSY_CAST`, which
// covered both "`=>` refuses this; write `=>!`" and "no spelling does this"
// under one code — so a log filtered on TYPE-009 could not separate the
// opt-out case from the impossible one, and the messages said opposite things
// about `=>!` under the same number. `cast_class`'s third answer gets its own.
pub func:TYPE_NO_CAST = string() never fails       { pass "NITPICK-TYPE-032"; };

// `for` OVER SOMETHING THAT DOES NOT ITERATE (D-166, 1.0.9). A range, a slice,
// an array, or — from 1.0.9d — a type implementing `Iterator`; anything else
// is refused HERE by name. It reached the backend, which refused it as a
// rung, and a rung names valid Nitpick.
pub func:TYPE_NOT_ITERABLE = string() never fails  { pass "NITPICK-TYPE-033"; };

// `==`/`!=` ON AN AGGREGATE (D-169, 1.0.9c). A struct, array, `Result`, `string`,
// `dyn`, two non-`NIL` `Optional`s or a payload-carrying enum has no built-in
// equality the language will pick a meaning for — derive or implement `Eq`. It
// reached the backend, which refused an `icmp` on an aggregate as a rung; the
// refusal is the checker's now, with the spelling that works. `== NIL` stays.
pub func:TYPE_NOT_COMPARABLE = string() never fails { pass "NITPICK-TYPE-034"; };

// A MODULE-LEVEL BINDING'S INITIALISER THAT IS NOT A COMPILE-TIME CONSTANT
// (D-165, 1.0.9c/1.0.9d). A call, or any expression the folder cannot fold, is
// refused: no module initialiser runs before `main`, so a value that had to run
// would appear in an order nobody wrote. It reached the backend as "a
// module-level binding" (a rung); the refusal is the checker's now, by name.
pub func:TYPE_GLOBAL_NOT_CONSTANT = string() never fails { pass "NITPICK-TYPE-035"; };

// AN INTERPOLATION `&{ x }` WHOSE TYPE HAS NO `ToString` (D-168, 1.0.9d). Inside
// a template a `string` renders as itself; anything else renders through the
// prelude trait `ToString`, and a type with no impl is refused by name — the
// backend cannot lower `&{ x }` for a value it has no `to_string` to call.
pub func:TYPE_NOT_STRINGABLE = string() never fails { pass "NITPICK-TYPE-036"; };

// D-163, `never fails`. A marked function's body may not `fail`, `relay`, or
// `return Result{…}`, and may not carry a `requires`/`ensures`/`limit`, the
// `async` modifier, or be `main`/`failsafe` — each is a way it could fail or a
// contract it cannot hold. One code, the message naming the offender.
pub func:TYPE_NEVER_FAILS_CAN_FAIL = string() never fails { pass "NITPICK-TYPE-037"; };
// A may-fail function value assigned where a `never fails` one is expected.
pub func:TYPE_NEVER_FAILS_ASSIGN = string() never fails { pass "NITPICK-TYPE-038"; };
// A `Result` (or any non-`NIL` value) discarded by a bare expression statement,
// with no keyword — rule 6's closed list.
pub func:TYPE_RESULT_DISCARDED = string() never fails { pass "NITPICK-TYPE-039"; };
// `fail` or `relay` inside a `defer` body (rule 7): a cleanup must not exit.
pub func:TYPE_DEFER_EXIT = string() never fails { pass "NITPICK-TYPE-040"; };
// An impl method dropping the `never fails` its trait declares.
pub func:TYPE_NEVER_FAILS_IMPL = string() never fails { pass "NITPICK-TYPE-041"; };
// D-163 rules 3-4, the licence itself (flipped on at 1.1.2): `raw` demands a
// call whose resolved callee declares `never fails`; `drop` additionally
// demands the success type `NIL` (a value is not "dropped", it is DISCARDED —
// `discard(raw f())` spells both claims). The spawn form is exempt: `drop` of
// an un-awaited `async` call is D-058's spawn, whose error the D-062 join
// delivers. One code; the message names the callee and the honest spellings.
pub func:TYPE_RAW_UNLICENSED = string() never fails { pass "NITPICK-TYPE-042"; };
// D-177 (1.1.4): `await` belongs to the coroutine machinery — legal only
// inside an `async` function, and only on a call to an `async` callee; the
// spawn form (`drop` of an un-awaited `async` call) likewise needs an
// enclosing `async` function, because the D-062 join lives in its frame.
pub func:TYPE_AWAIT_RULES = string() never fails { pass "NITPICK-TYPE-043"; };

// D-179: `failsafe`'s signature is fixed — exactly one parameter, typed
// `Error`. The runtime routes every uncaught failure through it, and the
// route has one shape.
pub func:TYPE_FAILSAFE_SIG = string() never fails { pass "NITPICK-TYPE-044"; };

// D-181: the `thread` modifier's rules. A thread body owns an executor, so
// it is an `async` function whose success is `NIL`, it is started by the
// spawn form, and neither `main` nor `failsafe` may be one.
pub func:TYPE_THREAD_RULES = string() never fails { pass "NITPICK-TYPE-045"; };

// D-183 §4: a type that OWNS something is move-only. Reading such a place in
// a value position copies it, and once drops exist a copy means two owners
// and one double free — so the transfer must be written, which is what makes
// `move` a fact rather than a decoration. A TEMPORARY needs none: there is no
// second name to invalidate.
pub func:TYPE_MOVE_REQUIRED = string() never fails { pass "NITPICK-TYPE-046"; };

// D-183: `move(p)` where `p` is an ordinary parameter of an owning type. The
// caller transferred nothing (D-065), so there is nothing here to transfer on
// — moving it would hand a second owner to whatever receives it while the
// caller still holds the first. Declaring the parameter `move` is how a
// function says it received ownership and may pass it along.
pub func:TYPE_MOVE_OF_BORROWED = string() never fails { pass "NITPICK-TYPE-047"; };

// 1.1.12b: asyncness is part of a trait method's contract. An `await`
// through a bound resolves the TRAIT's word and drives the impl through the
// coroutine frame protocol — a sync impl there would be run as a machine it
// is not, and an async impl of a sync method could never be awaited. Both
// directions refuse.
pub func:TYPE_ASYNC_IMPL = string() never fails { pass "NITPICK-TYPE-048"; };
